{"name":"Nepal","hex":"#93aab9","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Nepal","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A country in South Asia, located between China and India. Official name: Federal Democratic Republic of Nepal. Capital and largest city: Kathmandu."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Nepal"}],"entries":[{"title":"Nepal","blocks":[{"text":"Nepal, officially the Federal Democratic Republic of Nepal, is a landlocked country in South Asia. It is mainly situated in the Himalayas, but also includes parts of the Indo-Gangetic Plain. It borders the Tibet Autonomous Region of China to the north, and India to the south, east, and west, while it is narrowly separated from Bangladesh by the Siliguri Corridor, and from Bhutan by the Indian state of Sikkim. Nepal has a diverse geography, including fertile plains, subalpine forested hills, and eight of the world's ten highest mountains, including Mount Everest, the highest point above mean sea level on Earth. Kathmandu is the nation's capital and its largest city. Nepal is a multi-ethnic, multi-lingual, multi-religious, and multi-cultural sovereign state, with Nepali as the official language."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Emblem_of_Nepal.svg/330px-Emblem_of_Nepal.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nepal","wikidata":"Q837"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/N%C4%93p%C4%81la_in_the_Allahabad_Pillar_inscription.jpg/500px-N%C4%93p%C4%81la_in_the_Allahabad_Pillar_inscription.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/N%C4%93p%C4%81la_in_the_Allahabad_Pillar_inscription.jpg/500px-N%C4%93p%C4%81la_in_the_Allahabad_Pillar_inscription.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"\"Nēpāla\" in the late Brahmi script, in the Allahabad Pillar inscription of Samudragupta (350–375 CE).[17]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANapala_in_the_Allahabad_Pillar_inscription.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Changunarayan_photowalk-WLV-3897.jpg/500px-Changunarayan_photowalk-WLV-3897.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Changunarayan_photowalk-WLV-3897.jpg/1280px-Changunarayan_photowalk-WLV-3897.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Changunarayan_photowalk-WLV-3897.jpg/500px-Changunarayan_photowalk-WLV-3897.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" In the premises of the Changu Narayan Temple is a stone inscription dated 464 AD, the first in Nepal since the Ashoka inscription of Lumbini (c. 250 BC).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChangunarayan_photowalk-WLV-3897.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Sinja_Valley%2C_Karnali.jpg/500px-Sinja_Valley%2C_Karnali.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Sinja_Valley%2C_Karnali.jpg/1280px-Sinja_Valley%2C_Karnali.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Sinja_Valley%2C_Karnali.jpg/500px-Sinja_Valley%2C_Karnali.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sinja Valley, thought to be the place of origin of the Khasa Kingdom and the Nepali language.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASinja_Valley%2C_Karnali.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Patan_Durbar_Square-2644.jpg/500px-Patan_Durbar_Square-2644.jpg?utm